      "id": 1,
      "text": "Leonard, Missouri earned its name in 1941 when the military camp was christened for General Leonard Wood, former U.S. Army Chief of Staff.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fort_Leonard_Wood",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Fort Leonard Wood sits in the Missouri Ozarks and has trained soldiers since World War II, becoming a key U.S. Army training installation.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fort_Leonard_Wood",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Initially built to train infantry, the camp shifted in 1941 to an engineer replacement training center, shaping its long‑term mission.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fort_Leonard_Wood",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "During World War II, Italian and German prisoners of war were held at Fort Leonard Wood, a lesser‑known chapter of the base’s history.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fort_Leonard_Wood",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
